---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { PERSON_ID } from '../../lib/schema';

const service = {
	'@type': 'Service',
	'@id': 'https://okarthur.com/review/#service',
	name: 'Independent review',
	serviceType:
		'Independent review of an infrastructure decision before it is confirmed',
	description:
		'A second opinion on a digital, data or AI investment, or on a decision that commits more than one development. Fixed scope, a fixed end date, and one written opinion from someone with no stake in the outcome.',
	url: 'https://okarthur.com/review/',
	provider: { '@id': PERSON_ID },
	areaServed: [
		{ '@type': 'Country', name: 'United Arab Emirates' },
		{ '@type': 'Country', name: 'United Kingdom' },
		{ '@type': 'Place', name: 'Gulf Cooperation Council' },
	],
	audience: {
		'@type': 'Audience',
		audienceType:
			'Infrastructure owners, developers and major capital programmes',
	},
};

/* The section and the FAQPage node are both built from this, so the answers
   Google reads and the answers on the page cannot drift apart. */
const questions = [
	{
		q: 'Is this an audit?',
		a: "An audit tests compliance against a standard, while a review gives an opinion on whether a decision is supported by its evidence. That evidence is usually a familiar set of documents, such as a business case, a supplier's proposal, a pilot's results, an integration or handover plan and a set of benefits claims. The review tests whether those documents answer the questions the decision actually turns on. It carries no certification, and it can sit alongside formal assurance.",
	},
	{
		q: 'Can a decision that has already been made be reviewed?',
		a: 'A decision already made can still be reviewed, such as a completed supplier selection or a platform that has already been funded. The question changes from whether to commit to what must now be true for the investment to pay back, and the method stays the same.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Can you review a procurement before it goes to market?',
		a: 'Before the tender is issued is one of the most useful moments to look. By the time bids arrive, most of the outcome has been decided by what the tender asked for and how it will be scored. A review at this point examines the technical scope and the evaluation criteria, and whether the contract should be bundled or decoupled. It also checks whether what is being bought fits the architecture and the data it will have to work with. Fixing a tender costs far less than managing a wrong award.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Does this need new reporting from my teams?',
		a: 'The review works from the material each development already holds, so no team is asked to produce new reporting. Nothing has to leave your organisation, and the review can run on your own systems under your own confidentiality terms where that is simpler for you.',
	},
	{
		q: 'You spent years on the supplier side. How is the review independent?',
		a: 'For 16 years I helped build the evidence that sellers put in front of infrastructure owners. I know what that material is written to show, and which questions it is not built to answer. The independence is written into each engagement, with no delivery work behind the opinion and any interest declared before work starts.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Do you take supplier-side work?',
		a: 'I take no supplier-side work on anything under review. If a conflict cannot be cleanly separated, I decline the engagement and do not try to manage it.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What does it cost?',
		a: 'The fee is fixed, and I propose it after a first call. There is no published rate, because scope is agreed for each decision, but once the fee is agreed it does not change and there is no time-and-materials element.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Why one person and not a firm?',
		a: 'A firm that reviews a proposal is often also in a position to sell what comes after it, such as the implementation or the programme office that runs it. I have seen that arithmetic from the inside. A review here has no delivery team behind it and no follow-on work it is priced to win, so the opinion is the whole of what you are buying.',
	},
];

const faq = {
	'@type': 'FAQPage',
	'@id': 'https://okarthur.com/review/#faq',
	mainEntity: questions.map(({ q, a }) => ({
		'@type': 'Question',
		name: q,
		acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: a },
	})),
};
---

<Base
	title="Independent review"
	description="A second opinion on a decision in infrastructure, before you commit. Fixed scope, fixed end date, one written opinion."
	schema={[service, faq]}
>
	<div class="page-head">
		<h1 class="page-title">Independent review</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			A second opinion on a decision, before you commit.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="prose numbered">
		<p>
			Digital and AI investments are usually approved on evidence put together
			by people who gain from the approval. The supplier wants the contract
			and the delivery team wants the programme, and neither is being
			dishonest when the evidence they assemble shows what they need it to
			show. A review reads that evidence on behalf of the owner alone, and
			gives a written opinion on what it supports.
		</p>
		<p>
			A decision that reaches across more than one development has a
			different problem. Each development reports against its own budget and
			programme, and usually reports well. What the decision means for the
			others appears in nobody's report, and it tends to be found once it is
			too late to act on. The review sets the decision against the whole
			portfolio before it is confirmed.
		</p>

		<h2 id="when-it-applies">When it applies</h2>
		<p>The review lands at a decision point, of one of two kinds.</p>
		<h3>A digital or AI commitment</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				A supplier's proposal for a digital or AI system, before you accept
				it.
			</li>
			<li>
				A digital twin or data platform, before you fund it, or fund it
				again.
			</li>
			<li>A digital asset, before you accept handover into operation.</li>
		</ul>
		<h3>A decision that reaches across the portfolio</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				A change of plan at group level, such as a pause or a re-phasing,
				before it is confirmed and before anyone has set out what it frees up
				or holds up elsewhere.
			</li>
			<li>
				A business case approved some time ago, where an assumption it rested
				on, such as a utility connection or a handover date next door, has
				since moved.
			</li>
			<li>
				A committee choosing which phases or parcels to fund within a limit,
				where each case was made on its own and nobody has set them against
				each other.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="what-is-reviewed">What is reviewed</h2>
		<p>
			The review works from material that already exists, usually the
			business case, the supplier's proposal, the stated information
			requirements and whatever assurance evidence has been produced. It adds
			a small number of interviews, agreed and named before work starts.
		</p>
		<p>
			For a decision across developments, the material is the programme,
			contracts, commitments and correspondence that each development already
			holds, and nobody is asked to produce new reporting. Much of the value
			comes from reading those documents against each other. A business case
			that counts on a handover in March, set beside a programme that now
			shows September, is the kind of disagreement the review is there to
			find.
		</p>
		<p>The decision is then tested against five questions.</p>
		<ul>
			<li>What decision does this evidence actually support.</li>
			<li>What would have to be true for the claim to hold.</li>
			<li>What breaks it.</li>
			<li>What does it cost to keep true after year one.</li>
			<li>
				What else it commits, and the date after which that cannot be changed.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="what-you-receive">What you receive</h2>
		<p>
			You receive a single written opinion, short enough for a board to read
			and specific enough to act on. It sets out what the evidence supports
			and what it does not, and the conditions under which the investment can
			be defended. It then lists the risks that matter, ranked by severity.
			The opinion comes with a one-page summary you can lift into your own
			paper, and I present it once to the sponsor or the board.
		</p>
		<p>
			Where the decision crosses developments, the opinion also sets out what
			it commits elsewhere, by when, and which options are still open. It is
			written about the decision, and it does not apportion blame between
			teams.
		</p>
		<p>
			Every opinion carries a reliance statement on its front page, bounding
			it to the decision reviewed, the material examined and the date it was
			issued. The statement matters as much as the findings, because an
			opinion, like a digital twin, is <a
				href="/notes/what-makes-a-digital-twin-worth-relying-on/"
				>worth relying on only within stated limits</a
			>.
		</p>
		<figure class="figure">
			<svg
				viewBox="23 0 435 360"
				role="img"
